# Verifier

Import HostTools from "./host_tools.lg".
Import KernelReport from "./kernel_report.lg".
Import KernelVerdict from "./kernel_verdict.lg".
Import ProofDocument from "./proof_document.lg".
Import SubmissionPolicy from "./submission_policy.lg".
Import VerdictRecord from "./verdict_record.lg".
Import VernacularCommand from "./vernacular_command.lg".

The judgement of a submission, start to finish, cheapest first: read it, probe
every name it declares, check the assembled document with the kernel, apply the
acceptance policy. A worker runs this to learn how its attempt went; the trusted
verifier runs the same function on its own pinned toolchain before anything is
recorded as proved, so the two disagree only if their toolchains do.

## Note
What each name's probe found: the preamble then `Check <name>.`, run by the kernel.

## To probeNames (tc: Toolchain, preamble: Text, names: Seq of Text) -> Result of Seq of Text and Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for name in names:
        Inspect kernelRun(tc, nameProbeDocument(preamble, name)):
            When Err (e):
                Return a new Err with error e.
            When Ok (run):
                If run's timedOut:
                    Push "unknown" to out.
                Otherwise:
                    Push probeOutcome(run's exitCode, run's stdout, name) to out.
    Return a new Ok with value out.

## Note
The verdict the probes give a document's names, if they give one.

## To probeVerdict (tc: Toolchain, preamble: Text, names: Seq of Text, what: Text, boundBy: Text) -> Option of Verdict:
    Inspect probeNames(tc, preamble, names):
        When Err (e):
            Return some infrastructureVerdict(e).
        When Ok (outcomes):
            Return nameProbeVerdict(what, boundBy, names, outcomes).
    Return some infrastructureVerdict("unprobed").

## Note
The verdict on `submission` as a proof of the statement `statementDoc`.

## To judgeSubmission (tc: Toolchain, statementDoc: Text, goalName: Text, submission: Text) -> Verdict:
    Let proofName be proofNameFor(goalName).
    Let problems be submissionProblems(submission, proofName, goalName).
    If length of problems is greater than 0:
        Return refusedSubmissionVerdict(problems).
    Inspect probeVerdict(tc, statementDoc, submissionNames(submission), "submission", "the statement or the prelude"):
        When Some (v):
            Return v.
        When None:
            Inspect kernelRun(tc, assembleProofDocument(statementDoc, submission, proofName)):
                When Err (e):
                    Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
                When Ok (run):
                    Return kernelVerdict(run's exitCode, run's stdout, run's timedOut, proofName, goalName).
    Return infrastructureVerdict("unjudged").

## Note
The verdict on a statement before review: it reads as a statement, its names are
new to the prelude, it checks, and its goal is a proposition. `verified` here means
well-formed, never true; whether it says what the problem says is the review's.

## To judgeStatement (tc: Toolchain, statementDoc: Text, goalName: Text) -> Verdict:
    Let problems be statementProblems(statementDoc, goalName).
    If length of problems is greater than 0:
        Return newVerdict("rejected", 0, join(problems, "; "), [], "the statement was refused before the kernel saw it").
    Inspect probeVerdict(tc, "", submissionNames(statementDoc), "statement", "the prelude"):
        When Some (v):
            Return v.
        When None:
            Inspect kernelRun(tc, statementCheckDocument(statementDoc, goalName)):
                When Err (e):
                    Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
                When Ok (run):
                    Inspect parseKernelReport(run's stdout):
                        When Err (e):
                            Return infrastructureVerdict(e).
                        When Ok (report):
                            Let n be length of report's outputs.
                            If report's checked is equal to false:
                                Return newVerdict("rejected", report's failedBlock, report's error, [], "the kernel refused the statement").
                            If n is equal to 0 or item n of report's outputs is not equal to goalName + " : Prop":
                                Return newVerdict("rejected", n, "the goal is not a proposition", [], "the goal must be a Prop").
                            Return newVerdict("verified", 0, "", [], "the statement checks and its goal is a proposition").
    Return infrastructureVerdict("unjudged").
